16
00:00:26,910 --> 00:00:28,890
In this video I'll explain how buying

17
00:00:28,910 --> 00:00:29,820
a business works

18
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:32,400
why existing customers are worth paying for

19
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:34,440
how to read the numbers why owners

20
00:00:34,460 --> 00:00:36,690
sell and the biggest trap to avoid

21
00:00:40,501 --> 00:00:42,231
Buying a business means you take over

22
00:00:42,251 --> 00:00:43,781
something that's already running

23
00:00:44,091 --> 00:00:46,881
its customers its equipment its systems and

24
00:00:46,901 --> 00:00:48,101
usually its name

25
00:00:48,361 --> 00:00:49,991
You're not starting from zero

26
00:00:50,231 --> 00:00:51,751
You're starting from somewhere

27
00:00:51,991 --> 00:00:53,321
And it's more common than you might

28
00:00:53,341 --> 00:00:53,931
think

29
00:00:54,171 --> 00:00:56,831
Many small businesses change hands every year

30
00:00:57,081 --> 00:01:00,301
often between ordinary people not big corporations

31
00:01:00,531 --> 00:01:02,861
Businesses for sale are listed on marketplaces

32
00:01:02,881 --> 00:01:05,131
online through business brokers

33
00:01:05,351 --> 00:01:07,311
and sometimes just by word of mouth

34
00:01:07,331 --> 00:01:08,191
in your town

35
00:01:08,471 --> 00:01:09,991
How do people afford it

36
00:01:10,221 --> 00:01:12,011
Usually not with cash alone

37
00:01:12,261 --> 00:01:13,761
A buyer might put down a portion

38
00:01:13,781 --> 00:01:15,721
of the price often 10% or

39
00:01:15,741 --> 00:01:16,291
more

40
00:01:16,551 --> 00:01:18,721
In the United States many buyers use

41
00:01:18,741 --> 00:01:21,581
loans backed by the Small Business Administration

42
00:01:21,831 --> 00:01:23,801
And often the seller agrees to finance

43
00:01:23,821 --> 00:01:26,051
part of the deal themselves getting paid

44
00:01:26,071 --> 00:01:26,901
over time

45
00:01:27,141 --> 00:01:29,161
That's called seller financing and it's a

46
00:01:29,181 --> 00:01:30,051
good sign

47
00:01:30,291 --> 00:01:31,701
A seller who is willing to wait

48
00:01:31,721 --> 00:01:33,811
for their money is betting the business

49
00:01:33,831 --> 00:01:34,851
will keep working

50
00:01:38,672 --> 00:01:40,052
Why pay for a business when you

51
00:01:40,072 --> 00:01:41,452
could start one for less

52
00:01:41,702 --> 00:01:43,582
Because the hardest part of any business

53
00:01:43,602 --> 00:01:44,862
is getting customers

54
00:01:45,092 --> 00:01:47,442
and an existing business already has them

55
00:01:47,682 --> 00:01:49,172
Think about what a new business has

56
00:01:49,192 --> 00:01:50,102
on day one

57
00:01:50,362 --> 00:01:51,492
No customers

58
00:01:51,742 --> 00:01:52,832
No reviews

59
00:01:53,082 --> 00:01:54,812
No idea which ads work

60
00:01:55,042 --> 00:01:56,682
Every dollar of revenue has to be

61
00:01:56,702 --> 00:01:57,522
fought for

62
00:01:57,782 --> 00:01:59,852
An established business already has people who

63
00:01:59,872 --> 00:02:00,642
come back

64
00:02:00,862 --> 00:02:02,342
a reputation in the area

65
00:02:02,612 --> 00:02:04,892
staff who know the job suppliers and

66
00:02:04,912 --> 00:02:07,142
systems that have been tested for years

67
00:02:07,362 --> 00:02:09,212
That's what you're really buying proof

68
00:02:09,472 --> 00:02:11,552
Proof that people want this and proof

69
00:02:11,572 --> 00:02:12,742
that it can make money

70
00:02:12,972 --> 00:02:15,362
Starting from scratch you can spend years

71
00:02:15,382 --> 00:02:16,912
and a lot of savings just finding

72
00:02:16,932 --> 00:02:18,502
out whether your idea works

73
00:02:18,762 --> 00:02:20,312
Buying you pay for that answer up

74
00:02:20,332 --> 00:02:20,952
front

75
00:02:21,172 --> 00:02:22,442
It's not risk-free

76
00:02:22,662 --> 00:02:23,942
But it trades some of the biggest

77
00:02:23,962 --> 00:02:26,432
unknowns for things you can actually check

78
00:02:30,251 --> 00:02:32,001
To judge any business you need four

79
00:02:32,021 --> 00:02:32,671
words

80
00:02:32,941 --> 00:02:35,241
Revenue profit cash flow and debt

81
00:02:35,471 --> 00:02:37,161
Revenue is all the money coming in

82
00:02:37,181 --> 00:02:38,071
from sales

83
00:02:38,321 --> 00:02:40,361
Profit is what's left after expenses

84
00:02:40,601 --> 00:02:42,961
rent wages supplies utilities

85
00:02:43,211 --> 00:02:45,351
Cash flow is the actual money moving

86
00:02:45,371 --> 00:02:46,711
in and out of the bank account

87
00:02:46,921 --> 00:02:48,431
and it's what pays the bills

88
00:02:48,701 --> 00:02:50,911
A business can look profitable on paper

89
00:02:51,021 --> 00:02:52,641
and still run short of cash

90
00:02:52,901 --> 00:02:54,651
Debt is what the business owes

91
00:02:54,901 --> 00:02:57,321
Here's an example using simple numbers

92
00:02:57,591 --> 00:02:59,791
A laundromat brings in three hundred thousand

93
00:02:59,811 --> 00:03:01,441
dollars a year in revenue

94
00:03:01,741 --> 00:03:04,131
Expenses are $210,000

95
00:03:04,381 --> 00:03:06,391
That leaves about $90,000 in cash

96
00:03:06,411 --> 00:03:07,471
flow for the owner

97
00:03:07,741 --> 00:03:09,881
Small businesses often sell for a multiple

98
00:03:09,901 --> 00:03:11,061
of that cash flow

99
00:03:11,291 --> 00:03:13,761
commonly somewhere around 2 to 4 times

100
00:03:13,921 --> 00:03:15,241
depending on the business

101
00:03:15,501 --> 00:03:17,381
Say the price is two hundred seventy

102
00:03:17,401 --> 00:03:18,161
thousand

103
00:03:18,391 --> 00:03:20,211
You put 10% down and borrow

104
00:03:20,231 --> 00:03:21,851
the rest over 10 years at about

105
00:03:21,871 --> 00:03:23,161
10.5%

106
00:03:23,401 --> 00:03:25,311
Your loan payment is roughly three thousand

107
00:03:25,331 --> 00:03:26,671
three hundred dollars a month

108
00:03:26,881 --> 00:03:28,871
about $39,000 a year

109
00:03:29,131 --> 00:03:31,201
So that $90,000 becomes about fifty

110
00:03:31,221 --> 00:03:33,621
one thousand left to pay yourself before

111
00:03:33,641 --> 00:03:34,431
taxes

112
00:03:34,661 --> 00:03:36,181
That's why you always look at cash

113
00:03:36,201 --> 00:03:38,341
flow after debt not just the headline

114
00:03:38,361 --> 00:03:38,911
number

115
00:03:39,151 --> 00:03:40,631
And never trust a number you can't

116
00:03:40,651 --> 00:03:41,481
verify

117
00:03:41,721 --> 00:03:44,151
Ask for tax returns and bank statements

118
00:03:44,301 --> 00:03:45,711
not just a summary sheet

119
00:03:49,541 --> 00:03:51,271
So why would anyone sell a business

120
00:03:51,291 --> 00:03:52,221
that makes money

121
00:03:52,471 --> 00:03:54,291
More often than you'd think the reason

122
00:03:54,311 --> 00:03:55,071
is life

123
00:03:55,321 --> 00:03:57,951
Many owners are reaching retirement age and

124
00:03:57,971 --> 00:03:59,761
their kids don't want to take over

125
00:04:00,041 --> 00:04:01,701
Some are burned out after decades of

126
00:04:01,721 --> 00:04:02,661
long days

127
00:04:02,911 --> 00:04:05,201
Some have health problems are moving away

128
00:04:05,331 --> 00:04:06,831
or want to start something new

129
00:04:07,061 --> 00:04:08,221
For a buyer that can be an

130
00:04:08,241 --> 00:04:09,041
opportunity

131
00:04:09,311 --> 00:04:11,581
a solid business sold for an ordinary

132
00:04:11,601 --> 00:04:12,231
reason

133
00:04:12,461 --> 00:04:14,401
But some owners sell because they see

134
00:04:14,421 --> 00:04:15,381
trouble coming

135
00:04:15,621 --> 00:04:18,221
Maybe a big competitor is opening nearby

136
00:04:18,481 --> 00:04:20,311
Maybe the lease is ending or the

137
00:04:20,331 --> 00:04:22,151
equipment is worn out or sales have

138
00:04:22,171 --> 00:04:23,851
been sliding for two years

139
00:04:24,091 --> 00:04:25,741
That's why you always ask why they're

140
00:04:25,761 --> 00:04:26,361
selling

141
00:04:26,571 --> 00:04:28,221
and then check whether the numbers tell

142
00:04:28,241 --> 00:04:29,321
the same story

143
00:04:29,571 --> 00:04:31,581
Look at several years of results not

144
00:04:31,601 --> 00:04:32,651
just the best one

145
00:04:32,911 --> 00:04:34,681
A business that's shrinking can look cheap

146
00:04:34,701 --> 00:04:35,571
for a reason

147
00:04:39,392 --> 00:04:40,992
Here's the trap that catches a lot

148
00:04:41,012 --> 00:04:42,422
of first-time buyers

149
00:04:42,722 --> 00:04:44,822
a business that depends entirely on its

150
00:04:44,842 --> 00:04:45,322
owner

151
00:04:45,592 --> 00:04:47,462
Maybe the customers are loyal to the

152
00:04:47,482 --> 00:04:48,572
owner personally

153
00:04:48,822 --> 00:04:50,782
Maybe the owner does all the sales

154
00:04:50,942 --> 00:04:53,662
holds every important relationship and keeps all

155
00:04:53,682 --> 00:04:54,982
the know-how in their head

156
00:04:55,212 --> 00:04:57,012
When that person walks out the door

157
00:04:57,132 --> 00:04:58,892
the business can walk out with them

158
00:04:59,142 --> 00:05:00,062
So check

159
00:05:00,312 --> 00:05:01,942
Who brings in the customers

160
00:05:02,182 --> 00:05:03,792
Could the business run for two weeks

161
00:05:03,812 --> 00:05:04,702
without the owner

162
00:05:04,962 --> 00:05:07,382
Are there written processes trained employees and

163
00:05:07,402 --> 00:05:09,902
contracts or just habits and handshakes

164
00:05:10,142 --> 00:05:11,702
If a few big customers make up

165
00:05:11,722 --> 00:05:13,752
most of the revenue what happens if

166
00:05:13,772 --> 00:05:14,642
one leaves

167
00:05:14,872 --> 00:05:16,432
You can reduce this risk

168
00:05:16,672 --> 00:05:18,222
Ask the seller to stay on for

169
00:05:18,242 --> 00:05:20,592
a transition period to train you and

170
00:05:20,612 --> 00:05:22,382
introduce you to key customers

171
00:05:22,622 --> 00:05:24,832
Use seller financing so the seller has

172
00:05:24,852 --> 00:05:26,532
a reason to help you succeed

173
00:05:26,762 --> 00:05:28,642
Some deals tie part of the price

174
00:05:28,792 --> 00:05:30,492
to how the business performs after the

175
00:05:30,512 --> 00:05:31,122
sale

176
00:05:31,352 --> 00:05:32,092
And get help

177
00:05:32,302 --> 00:05:34,002
an accountant and a lawyer who review

178
00:05:34,022 --> 00:05:36,292
small business purchases can be worth every

179
00:05:36,312 --> 00:05:36,892
dollar

180
00:05:40,741 --> 00:05:42,121
So you don't have to invent the

181
00:05:42,141 --> 00:05:44,231
next big company to own a business

182
00:05:44,461 --> 00:05:46,421
You can buy one that already works

183
00:05:46,501 --> 00:05:48,201
if you understand what you're buying

184
00:05:48,431 --> 00:05:50,941
Know the difference between revenue profit cash

185
00:05:50,961 --> 00:05:51,971
flow and debt

186
00:05:52,211 --> 00:05:53,861
Ask why the owner is selling

187
00:05:54,121 --> 00:05:56,061
Make sure the business can survive without

188
00:05:56,081 --> 00:05:56,531
them

189
00:05:56,791 --> 00:05:58,341
And verify everything

190
00:05:58,581 --> 00:06:00,431
Buying a business isn't a shortcut to

191
00:06:00,451 --> 00:06:01,281
easy money

192
00:06:01,511 --> 00:06:03,141
It's a different starting line

193
00:06:03,381 --> 00:06:06,081
This video is for education not financial

194
00:06:06,101 --> 00:06:07,401
or legal advice

195
00:06:07,651 --> 00:06:09,051
Would you rather start a business from

196
00:06:09,071 --> 00:06:11,741
